== Chapter 10

#align(center)[IN SCRIPTURE, TO CORRECT ALL SUPERSTITION, THE TRUE GOD OPPOSED, EXCLUSIVELY, TO ALL THE GODS OF THE HEATHEN.]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[#emph[Sections (Beveridge’s summary).]]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[1\. The explanation of the knowledge of God resumed. God as revealed in Scripture is the same as he is portrayed in his works.]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[2\. The attributes of God as described by Moses, David, and Jeremiah. The attributes explained. Summary. The uses of this knowledge.]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[3\. Scripture, in directing us to the true God, excludes the gods of the heathen, though the heathen did in some sense hold the unity of God.]

1\. #smallcaps[We] noted earlier that the knowledge of God, which in other respects is set forth not obscurely in the frame of the world and in all created things, is explained more clearly and more intimately by the word. It may now be proper to show that in Scripture the Lord represents himself in the same character in which we have already seen him portrayed in his works. A full discussion of this subject would take up a great deal of space. But here it will be enough to provide a kind of index. By attending to it, the pious reader may learn what knowledge of God he should chiefly look for in Scripture, and be directed to a definite goal as he conducts the search. I am not yet touching on the special covenant by which God set the race of Abraham apart from the rest of the nations. For when by free adoption he admitted those who were enemies to the rank of sons, he was even then acting as a Redeemer. For the present, however, we are considering the knowledge that stops at the creation of the world and does not rise to Christ the Mediator. But although it will soon be necessary to quote certain passages from the New Testament (since proofs are given there both of the power of God the Creator and of his providence in preserving what he first created), I want the reader to remember what my present purpose is, so that he does not wander from the proper subject. In short, it will be enough for him for now to understand how God, the Creator of heaven and earth, governs the world he made. Throughout Scripture we find descriptions of his fatherly kindness and his readiness to do good, and we also find examples of severity which show that he is the just punisher of the wicked, especially when they remain obstinate in spite of all his forbearance.

2\. Certain passages contain more vivid descriptions of the divine character, setting it before us as if his true face were painted there for us to see. Moses, indeed, seems to have meant to sum up briefly whatever man may know of God, when he said, “The #smallcaps[Lord], the #smallcaps[Lord] God, is compassionate and gracious, slow to anger, abounding in goodness and truth, maintaining mercy to a thousand generations, forgiving iniquity, transgression, and sin. Yet He will by no means leave the guilty unpunished; He will visit the iniquity of the fathers on their children and grandchildren to the third and fourth generations” (Exod 34:6, 7). Here we should notice, #emph[first], that his eternity and self-existence are proclaimed by his magnificent name, repeated twice; and, #emph[second], that in the list of his perfections he is described not as he is in himself, but as he is toward us, so that our acknowledgment of him may consist more in a vivid, real sense of him than in empty, airy speculation. Moreover, the perfections listed here are just those we saw shining in the heavens and on the earth—compassion, goodness, mercy, justice, judgment, and truth. For power and might are included under the name Elohim. The prophets use the same titles when they want to declare his holy name in full. Not to gather a great number of passages, it may be enough for now to point to one Psalm (145), in which a summary of the divine perfections is given so carefully that not one seems to have been left out. And yet every perfection set down there may be contemplated in creation; so, such as experience teaches us that he is, such he declares himself to be by his word. In Jeremiah, where God proclaims the character in which he wants us to acknowledge him, the description is not so full, but it comes to the same thing. “Let him who glories,” he says, “glory in this, that he understands and knows Me, that I am the #smallcaps[Lord], who exercises mercy, judgment, and righteousness on the earth” (Jer 9:24). Certainly these three are the attributes we most need to know: mercy, on which alone the salvation of us all depends; judgment, which is exercised daily on the wicked and awaits them in a heavier form still, for their eternal destruction; and righteousness, by which the faithful are preserved and most kindly cherished. The prophet declares that when you have grasped these, you are amply supplied with the means of glorying in God. Yet his truth, his power, his holiness and his goodness are not left out here. For how could this knowledge of his mercy, judgment, and righteousness stand, if it did not rest on his inviolable truth? Again, how could anyone believe that he governs the earth with judgment and righteousness without presupposing his mighty power? And where does his mercy come from, if not from his goodness? Finally, if all his ways are mercy, judgment, and righteousness, his holiness too is plainly seen in them. Moreover, the knowledge of God set before us in the Scriptures is meant for the same purpose as the knowledge that shines in creation: namely, to invite us first to the fear of God, and then to trust in him, so that by it we may learn to worship him with perfect innocence of life and sincere obedience, and also to depend wholly on his goodness.

3\. Here it may be well to give a summary of the general doctrine. First, then, let the reader notice that Scripture, in order to direct us to the true God, expressly excludes and rejects all the gods of the heathen, because religion had been corrupted far and wide in almost every age. It is true, indeed, that the name of one God was known and celebrated everywhere. For those who worshiped a multitude of gods, whenever they spoke the genuine language of nature, simply used the name “god”, as if they were content with one god. Justin Martyr wisely noticed this, and to that end wrote a treatise, entitled On the Monarchy of God, in which he shows by a great variety of evidence that the unity of God is engraved on the hearts of all. Tertullian also proves the same thing from the common ways of speaking.#footnote[In his book De Idolatria \[On Idolatry\]. See also, in Augustine, a letter by one Maximus, a grammarian of Medaura, joking about his gods and scoffing at the true religion. See at the same time Augustine’s grave and admirable reply. Ep. 43. 44. (Beveridge’s note.)] But all without exception, in the futility of their minds, have rushed or been dragged into lying fictions, and so their understanding has become futile. So these impressions of the unity of God, whatever they may naturally have been, have done nothing more than leave men inexcusable. Even the wisest plainly reveal the aimless wandering of their minds when they wish for some god to help them, and so offer up their prayers to uncertain gods. And then, in imagining a manifold nature in God, though their ideas about Jupiter, Mercury, Venus, Minerva, and the others were not so absurd as those of the ignorant common people, they themselves were by no means free from the delusions of the devil. We have observed elsewhere that, however subtle the evasions the philosophers devised, they cannot clear themselves of the charge of rebellion, because all of them have corrupted the truth of God. For this reason Habakkuk (2:20), after condemning all idols, commands men to seek God in his temple, so that the faithful may acknowledge none but Him who has revealed himself in his word.
